---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="About — Cassandra Open Education"
  description="Why Cassandra exists, what changed between 2019 and now, and how The Muses non-profit runs it."
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h1>About Cassandra</h1>

      <p class="lede">
        Cassandra is a free, open guide through the world's free educational resources, run by
        volunteers and owned by no one's business model.
      </p>

      <h2>The idea</h2>
      <p>
        An extraordinary thing happened over the last two decades: most of what you need to learn a
        profession was published online, free. University lecture halls, interactive curricula,
        world-class textbooks — Harvard's CS50, MIT OpenCourseWare, freeCodeCamp, Khan Academy,
        OpenStax and hundreds more.
      </p>
      <p>
        But abundance created its own barrier. A learner starting from nothing faces ten thousand
        free courses and no way to know which are good, what order they go in, or when they've
        learned enough to apply for a job. People with educated families, mentors or money get that
        map from other humans. Everyone else gets decision paralysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        Cassandra is the map, given away. Ordered paths through the best free material, an honest
        account of the effort involved, and a checkpoint at every stage so learners finish with
        proof — a portfolio — rather than only certificates.
      </p>

      <h2>The story: 2019 to now</h2>
      <p>
        Cassandra began in 2019 as a more ambitious and more conventional platform: a database-backed
        aggregator where users would post, vote on and review educational resources. Volunteer
        developers built a working prototype before the project went quiet — you can still see it in
        this repository's history.
      </p>
      <p>
        When we returned to it, we asked the honest question: does the world still need that? The
        answer was no. Search engines, established course directories and now AI assistants have
        solved <em>finding</em> a resource. What they still don't give a learner is a trustworthy,
        human-curated <em>route</em> — and that is a problem better solved by an open, reviewable
        text repository than by a database with accounts. So Cassandra was rebuilt from scratch
        around that: no backend, no logins, no votes. Just paths, maintained in the open like the
        best community curricula, reviewed change by change.
      </p>

      <h2>What Cassandra deliberately is not</h2>
      <p>
        We don't host courses — the providers we link to do that far better than we could. We don't
        issue certificates — our checkpoints produce portfolio work instead, which employers trust
        more than unaccredited paper. And we don't collect users — there is nothing to sign up for,
        and your progress is stored only on your own device.
      </p>

      <h2>About The Muses</h2>
      <p>
        The Muses is a non-profit organisation registered in the United Kingdom, aimed at addressing
        the economic barriers to education and the socio-economic problems that follow from them. It
        has a legally binding fiduciary responsibility to provide social welfare only, and is
        prohibited from generating income for any directors or managers — everyone involved donates
        their time.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our ideal is an education system where the resources belong to everyone, and institutions
        exist to guide learners through them — not to gate them. Cassandra is one small, practical
        piece of that.
      </p>

      <p>
        <a class="button" href="/contribute">Help build it</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
